body:
Let $I$ be a directed set. Let $(L_i, \lambda_{ij})$, $(M_i, \mu_{ij})$, and $(N_i, 
u_{ij})$ be systems of $R$-modules over $I$. Let $\varphi_i : L_i 	o M_i$ and $\psi_i : M_i 	o N_i$ be morphisms of systems over $I$. Assume that for all $i \in I$ the sequence of $R$-modules $$ \xymatrix{ L_i \ar[r]^{\varphi_i} & M_i \ar[r]^{\psi_i} & N_i } $$ is a complex with homology $H_i$. Then the $R$-modules $H_i$ form a system over $I$, the sequence of $R$-modules $$ \xymatrix{ \mathop{\mathrm{colim}}
olimits_i L_i \ar[r]^\varphi & \mathop{\mathrm{colim}}
olimits_i M_i \ar[r]^\psi & \mathop{\mathrm{colim}}
olimits_i N_i } $$ is a complex as well, and denoting $H$ its homology we have $$ H = \mathop{\mathrm{colim}}
olimits_i H_i. $$

Let $A 	o B$ be a ring map. Let $g \in B$. Suppose $\alpha : P 	o B$ is a presentation with kernel $I$. Then a presentation of $B_g$ over $A$ is the map $$  eta : P[x] \longrightarrow B_g $$ extending $\alpha$ and sending $x$ to $1/g$. The kernel $J$ of $ eta$ is generated by $I$ and the element $f x - 1$ where $f \in P$ is an element mapped to $g \in B$ by $\alpha$. In this situation we have  egin{enumerate} \item $J/J^2 = (I/I^2)_g \oplus B_g (f x - 1)$, \item $\Omega_{P[x]/A} \otimes_{P[x]} B_g = \Omega_{P/A} \otimes_P B_g \oplus B_g 	ext{d}x$, \item $\mathop{N\!L}
olimits( eta) \cong \mathop{N\!L}
olimits(\alpha) \otimes_B B_g \oplus (B_g \xrightarrow{g} B_g)$ \end{enumerate} Hence the canonical map $\mathop{N\!L}
olimits_{B/A} \otimes_B B_g 	o \mathop{N\!L}
olimits_{B_g/A}$ is a homotopy equivalence.

[Yoneda]   Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a category, and $X, Y \in \mathop{\mathrm{Ob}}
olimits(\mathcal{C})$. There is a natural bijection $$  egin{matrix} \mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}
olimits_\mathcal{C}(X, Y) & \longrightarrow & \mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}
olimits_{	extit{PSh}(\mathcal{C})} (h_X, h_Y) \ \psi & \longmapsto & h_\psi = \psi \circ - : h_X 	o h_Y. \end{matrix} $$

Let $I$ be a finitely generated ideal of a ring $A$. The inclusion functor $D_{comp}(A, I) 	o D(A)$ has a left adjoint, i.e., given any object $K$ of $D(A)$ there exists a map $K 	o K^\wedge$ of $K$ into a derived complete object of $D(A)$ such that the map $$ \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}
olimits_{D(A)}(K^\wedge, E) \longrightarrow \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}
olimits_{D(A)}(K, E) $$ is bijective whenever $E$ is a derived complete object of $D(A)$. In fact, if $I$ is generated by $f_1, \ldots, f_r \in A$, then we have $$ K^\wedge = R\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}
olimits\left((A 	o \prod
olimits_{i_0} A_{f_{i_0}} 	o \prod
olimits_{i_0 < i_1} A_{f_{i_0}f_{i_1}} 	o \ldots 	o A_{f_1\ldots f_r}), Kight) $$ functorially in $K$.

Given any glueing data of locally ringed spaces there exists a locally ringed space $X$ and open subspaces $U_i \subset X$ together with isomorphisms $\varphi_i : X_i 	o U_i$ of locally ringed spaces such that  egin{enumerate} \item $X= igcup_{i\in I} U_i$, \item $\varphi_i(U_{ij}) = U_i \cap U_j$, and \item $\varphi_{ij} = \varphi_j^{-1}|_{U_i \cap U_j} \circ \varphi_i|_{U_{ij}}$. \end{enumerate} The locally ringed space $X$ is characterized by the following mapping properties: Given a locally ringed space $Y$ we have  egin{eqnarray*} \mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}
olimits(X, Y) & = & \{ (f_i)_{i\in I} \mid f_i : X_i 	o Y, \ f_j \circ \varphi_{ij} = f_i|_{U_{ij}}\} \ f & \mapsto & (f|_{U_i} \circ \varphi_i)_{i \in I} \ \mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}
olimits(Y, X) & = & \left\{  egin{matrix} 	ext{open covering }Y =  igcup
olimits_{i \in I} V_i	ext{ and } (g_i : V_i 	o X_i)_{i \in I} 	ext{ such that}\ g_i^{-1}(U_{ij}) = V_i \cap V_j 	ext{ and } g_j|_{V_i \cap V_j} = \varphi_{ij} \circ g_i|_{V_i \cap V_j} \end{matrix} ight\} \ g & \mapsto & V_i = g^{-1}(U_i), \ g_i = \varphi_i^{-1} \circ g|_{V_i} \end{eqnarray*}

Let $(\mathcal{C}, \mathcal{O})$ be a ringed site. Given $K, L, M$ in $D(\mathcal{O})$ there is a canonical morphism $$ R\mathop{\mathcal{H}\!\mathit{om}}
olimits(L, M) \otimes_\mathcal{O}^\mathbf{L} R\mathop{\mathcal{H}\!\mathit{om}}
olimits(K, L) \longrightarrow R\mathop{\mathcal{H}\!\mathit{om}}
olimits(K, M) $$ in $D(\mathcal{O})$.

Let $u : \mathcal{C} 	o \mathcal{D}$, and $v : \mathcal{D} 	o \mathcal{E}$ be cocontinuous functors. Then $v \circ u$ is cocontinuous and we have $h = g \circ f$ where $f : \mathop{\mathit{Sh}}
olimits(\mathcal{C}) 	o \mathop{\mathit{Sh}}
olimits(\mathcal{D})$, resp.\ $g : \mathop{\mathit{Sh}}
olimits(\mathcal{D}) 	o \mathop{\mathit{Sh}}
olimits(\mathcal{E})$, resp.\ $h : \mathop{\mathit{Sh}}
olimits(\mathcal{C}) 	o \mathop{\mathit{Sh}}
olimits(\mathcal{E})$ is the morphism of topoi associated to $u$, resp.\ $v$, resp.\ $v \circ u$.

Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a site. Let $\mathcal{F}$, $\mathcal{G}$ and $\mathcal{H}$ be sheaves on $\mathcal{C}$. There is a canonical bijection $$ \mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}
olimits_{\mathop{\mathit{Sh}}
olimits(\mathcal{C})}(\mathcal{F}	imes\mathcal{G},\mathcal{H}) = \mathop{\mathrm{Mor}}
olimits_{\mathop{\mathit{Sh}}
olimits(\mathcal{C})}(\mathcal{F},\mathop{\mathcal{H}\!\mathit{om}}
olimits(\mathcal{G},\mathcal{H})) $$ which is functorial in all three entries.
